# cluster/inventory.env — allocation description consumed by every cluster/*.sh
# Copy to inventory.env and edit for a new allocation. No secrets live here
# (SSH keys stay in ~/.ssh; only aliases are referenced).
#
# This demo runs on a SINGLE Intel Xeon 6+ instance. NODE_HOSTS / NODE_JUMPS are
# one-element arrays index-aligned with NODES.

# --- Starting path selector -----------------------------------------------------
# icloud   (default) = request a bare-metal Xeon 6+ node from Intel Cloud
#                      Services, which hands you a clean Ubuntu 26.04 image and
#                      jump-host SSH (Skills 1 / 1b / 1c).
# baremetal          = you already have root on your own bare-metal Clearwater
#                      Forest node; re-image it IN PLACE to a clean Ubuntu 26.04
#                      and enable key SSH with cluster/10-baremetal-provision.sh
#                      (Skill 1-BM). The two paths converge at the kernel build.
#
# Already have a node you do NOT want to re-image? There is no third mode: keep
# PROVISION_MODE=icloud, skip Stage 1 (do NOT run request/allocate or the
# re-image script), fill in the direct-SSH block below (NODE_JUMPS=('') ), and
# start the toolkit at the first stage you actually need:
#   * Non-Ubuntu node, want an AET kernel  -> set KERNEL_SOURCE=git,
#     KERNEL_PKG=rpm, KERNEL_BRANCH=<AET tag>; start at `20-kernel-build.sh`,
#     then `21-kernel-install.sh` (ship/install/promote), then `aet-check.sh`.
#   * Ubuntu node on an older kernel        -> keep KERNEL_SOURCE=ubuntu; start
#     at `20-kernel-build.sh` exactly as the icloud path does.
#   * Node ALREADY running an AET kernel    -> skip the kernel stages entirely;
#     start at `aet-check.sh` to confirm the counters, then `30-k3s-up.sh`.
# See the README "Where do you start?" table for the full decision matrix.
PROVISION_MODE=icloud

# --- Node (SSH alias; must resolve in your ~/.ssh/config) ----------------------
NODES=(icloud-aet0)                        # exactly one node (the k3s server)

# The node must be a bare-metal Xeon 6+ (e.g. BM-CWF) or the AET counters are
# absent. 00-probe.sh prints the node's `Model name`; set this to also assert
# the model the catalog promised (substring match, empty = report-only).
EXPECT_CPU_MODEL=""

# --- SSH ------------------------------------------------------------------------
SSH_OPTS="-o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=30 -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new"

# Connection facts from the portal's per-instance SSH panel, used by
# cluster/01-ssh-config.sh to generate (and later remove) the ~/.ssh/config
# stanza for this allocation. Index-aligned with NODES.
SSH_IDENTITY=~/.ssh/id_ed25519_intelcloud  # private key whose .pub you uploaded
NODE_USER=devcloud                         # login user shown by the SSH action
NODE_HOSTS=(192.168.4.2)                   # the node's IP
NODE_JUMPS=(146.152.205.46)                # the node's jump host ('' = direct)
JUMP_USER=guest                            # login user on the jump host

# For PROVISION_MODE=baremetal the node is reached DIRECTLY on your lab network,
# so there is no jump host. Set the SSH block like this instead (NODE_USER is the
# login user cluster/10-baremetal-provision.sh CREATES via autoinstall, and the
# .pub of SSH_IDENTITY is injected into its authorized_keys — do NOT use
# devcloud here):
#   NODES=(aet-baremetal0)
#   NODE_USER=<your-login-user>
#   NODE_HOSTS=(10.20.30.40)               # the node's real lab IP
#   NODE_JUMPS=('')                        # '' = direct route, no jump host
#   SSH_IDENTITY=~/.ssh/id_ed25519_intelcloud
# The PROXY_* block below is reused unchanged on the Intel network.

# --- Bare-metal re-image (only when PROVISION_MODE=baremetal) --------------------
# cluster/10-baremetal-provision.sh re-images a node you already own, IN PLACE,
# by kexec-ing its running OS into the Ubuntu 26.04 live-server installer driven
# unattended by a NoCloud autoinstall seed served over HTTP from this
# workstation. This WIPES the node's target disk — see the README "Alternative
# start" section and keep an out-of-band console/BMC/KVM recovery path.
UBUNTU_ISO_URL=https://releases.ubuntu.com/26.04/ubuntu-26.04-live-server-amd64.iso
UBUNTU_ISO_SHA256=                          # from the release SHA256SUMS; verified on `fetch`
REIMAGE_HOSTNAME=aet-baremetal0            # hostname for the clean install
REIMAGE_TARGET_DISK=                       # e.g. /dev/nvme0n1; empty = auto-detect on `capture` then confirm
REIMAGE_NET=static                         # static (preserve the captured IP) | dhcp (needs a MAC reservation)

# Storage strategy:
#   direct (default) = wipe the WHOLE REIMAGE_TARGET_DISK (destroys every OS on it).
#   reuse            = keep every OTHER partition (e.g. a Windows dual-boot and a
#                      shared ESP) and reformat ONLY the partitions named below.
#                      `capture` records the exact geometry; the ESP is reused
#                      (mounted at /boot/efi, never reformatted) so a shared
#                      Windows/Ubuntu ESP survives.
REIMAGE_STORAGE=direct
# reuse-mode partitions (only read when REIMAGE_STORAGE=reuse):
REIMAGE_DISK=                              # whole disk holding the partitions, e.g. /dev/nvme0n1
REIMAGE_ESP_PART=                          # existing EFI System Partition to reuse, e.g. /dev/nvme0n1p2
REIMAGE_BOOT_PART=                         # partition reformatted ext4 as /boot, e.g. /dev/nvme0n1p5
REIMAGE_ROOT_PART=                         # partition reformatted ext4 as /,    e.g. /dev/nvme0n1p6
SEED_HTTP_BIND=                            # this workstation's lab-facing IP the node can reach (empty = auto-detect)
SEED_HTTP_PORT=8099                        # HTTP port serving the ISO + NoCloud seed to the node
SEED_BASE_URL=                             # optional: serve via an existing/external HTTP server instead of the
                                           # built-in one. Set to the base URL exposing ./iso/ and ./seed/
                                           # (e.g. http://10.0.0.5/~user/ab). When set, 'reimage' builds the
                                           # kexec URLs from it and does not require a local 'serve'. Use this
                                           # in locked-down labs where the node can only reach a shared web host.
# Secure Boot must be OFF in firmware (the rebuilt AET kernel is unsigned);
# autoinstall cannot toggle it. Confirm in BIOS/BMC before re-imaging.

# --- k3s ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pin the k3s release cluster/30-k3s-up.sh installs. The installer downloads and
# sha256-verifies the matching k3s binary over TLS; pinning makes that binary
# deterministic. Bump to a release you have tested for this hardware.
# v1.36.2+k3s1 (containerd 2.3.2-k3s2) is the release validated end-to-end on
# Intel Cloud Xeon 6+ / Ubuntu 26.04 with NRI enabled.
INSTALL_K3S_VERSION=v1.36.2+k3s1

# --- Corporate proxy (only if this workstation is behind a firewall) ------------
# Set these and run `cluster/05-proxy-setup.sh` to configure git/curl, the Docker
# daemon + build client, and SSH-to-jump-host in one go. Leave PROXY_URL empty if
# the workstation has direct outbound internet. See README "Working behind a
# corporate firewall" for the one-time steps.
# PROXY_URL=http://proxy.example.com:912   # HTTP proxy for http_proxy AND https_proxy
# PROXY_NO_PROXY=                          # override the computed no_proxy (loopback + node/cluster CIDRs)
# PROXY_JUMP_HOST=                         # ssh host/alias to attach the ProxyCommand to (the cloud jump host)
# PROXY_SSH_TYPE=http                      # how ssh tunnels through the proxy: http | socks5
# PROXY_SSH_HOSTPORT=                      # host:port for the ssh tunnel (default: derived from PROXY_URL)
# PROXY_CA=/etc/ssl/certs/your-corp-root-ca.pem  # only if the proxy re-signs TLS (MITM inspection)
# PROXY_GOPROXY=                           # internal Go module proxy if proxy.golang.org is blocked

# --- Grafana publishing (cluster/70-grafana-tunnel.sh) --------------------------
# Expose the in-cluster Grafana (HTTP NodePort 30030) as an HTTPS URL a user can
# open, via a persistent TLS reverse proxy on a public-facing PUBLISH_HOST plus
# an SSH tunnel. Leave PUBLISH_HOST empty to use only the operator-local view
# (`70-grafana-tunnel.sh local` -> http://localhost:3000).
PUBLISH_HOST=                              # public-facing host running the TLS proxy (e.g. a bastion)
GRAFANA_HTTPS_PORT=3443                    # TLS proxy port on PUBLISH_HOST (browser connects here)
GRAFANA_BACKEND_PORT=3008                  # plain-HTTP backend on PUBLISH_HOST the tunnel binds
GRAFANA_NODEPORT=30030                     # Grafana HTTP NodePort in the cluster
# GRAFANA_NODE_TARGET=192.168.4.2:30030    # host:port the workstation dials for the reverse tunnel
#                                          # (default: the node's reachable IP:GRAFANA_NODEPORT)
# GRAFANA_ROOT_URL=                        # public URL browsers use (must match the origin).
#                                          # Default when PUBLISH_HOST is set:
#                                          # https://PUBLISH_HOST:GRAFANA_HTTPS_PORT/ . Set this
#                                          # only to override (e.g. a DNS name or port 443).
#                                          # A wrong value breaks Grafana auth-token rotation
#                                          # (CSRF/Origin check) -> sessions drop -> "No data".
# Grafana admin credentials. 40-deploy-telemetry.sh creates a grafana-admin
# Secret from these; the manifest reads the password only from that Secret
# (never a literal). Leave GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD empty to have the deploy
# generate a random password and print it once. Anonymous access is disabled.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin                   # Grafana admin username
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=                    # empty -> random password generated at deploy and printed once

# --- Kernel (rebuild the node's own Ubuntu kernel with AET enabled) -------------
# cluster/20-kernel-build.sh rebuilds the kernel the node already runs, using the
# containerized build system bundled under ../kernel (KERNEL_BUILD_DIR). The
# source defaults to Ubuntu's own `linux` source package, pinned to the version
# the node boots, so Ubuntu's patches and its LTS security-update commitment are
# preserved and the only delta is enabling CONFIG_X86_CPU_RESCTRL_INTEL_AET.
KERNEL_SOURCE=ubuntu               # ubuntu = Ubuntu's linux source package | git = a mainline tag
KERNEL_PKG=auto                    # deb | rpm | auto — package format built for and installed on the node.
                                   # auto = detect from the node on `capture` (dpkg->deb, rpm->rpm) and
                                   # remember it. deb = Debian/Ubuntu; rpm = RHEL/Rocky/Fedora. An rpm
                                   # node has no Ubuntu source package, so KERNEL_PKG=rpm REQUIRES
                                   # KERNEL_SOURCE=git (a mainline tag that carries AET). The .deb build
                                   # runs in ubuntu-build.Docker, the .rpm build in rocky-build.Docker;
                                   # the build host only needs Docker either way. On an rpm node the
                                   # install/promote steps use grubby + grub2-reboot instead of update-grub.
KERNEL_TAG=7.0.12-aet              # release of the kernel you will boot; MUST match what the build
                                   # produces. It follows the UPSTREAM POINT RELEASE of the Ubuntu
                                   # source (e.g. Ubuntu 7.0.0-29.29 is upstream v7.0.12 -> 7.0.12-aet),
                                   # so it moves with each Ubuntu update. `verify` reports the exact
                                   # value and fails if this disagrees.
STOCK_KERNEL=                      # node's current (stock) kernel — REQUIRED, set this yourself from the
                                   # `uname -r` that `00-probe.sh` reports. It is NOT filled in
                                   # automatically (`capture` only writes a reminder file), and it is the
                                   # boot-default fallback, so `21-kernel-install.sh install` fails if empty.
KERNEL_BUILD_HOST=localhost        # host with Docker that runs the build (usually your workstation)
KERNEL_BUILD_DIR=$CLUSTER_DIR/../kernel   # bundled containerized kernel build system
KERNEL_LOCALVERSION=-aet           # appended to the built kernel version
KERNEL_CMDLINE_ADD="rdt=perf"      # AET requires rdt=perf on the kernel command line
# KERNEL_SLIM_DEBUG=1              # faster build, no huge -dbg package, but DROPS BTF (Ubuntu ships it on)

# KERNEL_SOURCE=ubuntu: `capture` auto-detects the series from the node. The
# build tracks the archive's CURRENT linux source (the fully patched one), since
# Ubuntu removes superseded versions — pin one only if you have a snapshot mirror.
# UBUNTU_KERNEL_VERSION=7.0.0-29.29  # pin an exact `linux` source version (default: archive's current)
# UBUNTU_SERIES=resolute             # node's Ubuntu codename
# UBUNTU_VERSION_ID=26.04            # node's Ubuntu version (selects the build base image)
# KERNEL_BUILD_IMAGE=ubuntu:26.04    # override the base image (add @sha256:... to pin it)

# KERNEL_SOURCE=git only — build a mainline tag instead of Ubuntu's source.
KERNEL_REPO=https://github.com/torvalds/linux.git  # public kernel source to clone in the build container
KERNEL_BRANCH=v7.1                 # tag/branch that carries AET resctrl support
# Optional: on a firewalled build host, serve a local clone instead of cloning KERNEL_REPO:
# KERNEL_LOCAL_REPO=~/src/linux

# --- nri-resctrl-mon image (cluster/telemetry/build-nri-image.sh) ----------------
# The per-Pod AET plugin (nri-resctrl-mon) and the resctrl monitor + OTel
# collector it depends on are not yet upstream, so the image is built from these
# public forks/branches. build-nri-image.sh clones them on demand; point
# NRI_SRC / GORESCTRL_SRC at existing checkouts to build from local trees instead.
NRI_REPO=https://github.com/cmcantalupo/nri-plugins.git   # nri-plugins fork carrying nri-resctrl-mon
NRI_BRANCH=resctrl-mon-goresctrl                          # branch with the goresctrl-based plugin
GORESCTRL_REPO=https://github.com/cmcantalupo/goresctrl.git  # goresctrl fork with pkg/monitor + OTel
GORESCTRL_BRANCH=resctrl-mon                              # branch with the resctrl monitor library
# Optional: build from existing local checkouts instead of cloning:
# NRI_SRC=~/Git/nri-plugins
# GORESCTRL_SRC=~/Git/goresctrl
